# Content licence

The **code** in this repository is MIT — see `LICENSE`. Take it, fork it, build
your own site with it. That is the half of this repository that is meant to
travel.

The **content** is not MIT, and the split is deliberate.

> **This is an open decision, not a settled one.** See MIGRATION.md, phase 0.
> Until it is settled, the conservative position below applies, because it is
> the only one that can be loosened later without asking 54 posts' worth of
> readers to unlearn something. A licence can be widened; it cannot be
> narrowed.

## Everything under `src/content/`

**© Kenny Baas-Schwegler. All rights reserved.**

The blog posts, talk write-ups and training descriptions are the working
material of a consultancy. They are published to be *read, linked and quoted*,
and quoting with attribution is fair dealing and needs no permission from
anyone. Republishing them wholesale is a different thing, and the answer is
"ask" rather than "no".

This is the one place Weave IT differs sharply from virtualddd.com, whose
content is CC BY-SA because it is community-authored and meant to be adapted.
Nothing here is community-authored.

**Answer engines are explicitly welcome**, and `public/robots.txt` says so by
name. "All rights reserved" is a statement about republication, not about being
read; a site nobody can find is not protecting anything.

## Images

Photographs, conference pictures, book covers and the Weave IT brand assets are
**not** covered by either licence. Several are other people's — a conference's
own photography, a publisher's cover art — and are used here by permission or
by fair dealing. Ask before reusing any of them.

## Third-party material

Where a page quotes or reproduces somebody else's work — a canvas, a diagram, a
passage from a book — that material stays under its own licence and the page
says whose it is. Nothing in this file overrides that.
